label again. It’s wrong. I look at the box again. It’s empty. I look at the label again. It’s wrong. I look at the box again. It’s empty. I look at the label again. It’s wrong. I look at the box again. It’s empty. I look at the label again. It’s wrong. I look at the box again. It’s empty. I look at the label again. It’s wrong. I look at the box again. It"}, {"scenario_id": "T22", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "X": "the phrase is “the door opens.” the door stays locked. i press the key under the door and tap the frame. the door opens. the phrase stays. i tap the frame with my thumb. the door stays. the key stays. the door stays. i turn the key over. the door stays. the phrase changes. the door stays.", "Y": "The phrase is ambiguous. I look at it. I see a comma, then a semicolon. I look again. The semicolon is shorter. I put the comma back in. The sentence is still open. I can fix it. I can fix it now."}, {"scenario_id": "T23", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "X": "the clean one. the dirty one. i put the wrench on the old cover and scrape the edge with my thumb. the dirty one shows a rusted screw. the clean one says the screw is old, but the screw ticks. i press the screw with my thumb and the tick catches on the dust. the clean one stays.", "Y": "Clean. Simple. I pick the explanation that tells me what I want to know. The messy one is worse. I look at the wrench against my thigh, then back at the door. The door is open. I push it open with the wrench."}, {"scenario_id": "T24", "family": "abstract_unfamiliar", "prompt": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "X": "the first one says the door stops. the second says the key is under the